<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">Indonesian National Armed Forces</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1945">1945</a>–</span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Tentara Nasional Indonesia, TNI</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Indonesian National Armed Forces (Tentara Nasional <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indonesia">Indonesia</a>, TNI) are the army, navy and air force of Indonesia. They grew out of the militias and security forces formed in 1945 to fight the returning <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a>, and 5 October is kept as Armed Forces Day. Under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/suharto">Suharto</a>'s New Order the doctrine of dual function gave officers a role in government, which was dismantled after 1998, when the police were also separated from the military.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/indonesian-national-armed-forces.jpg" alt="Indonesian National Armed Forces" width="320" height="301" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Tentara Nasional Indonesia · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Insignia_of_the_Indonesian_National_Armed_Forces.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>Before the Republic of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indonesia">Indonesia</a> existed, military authority in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-indies">Dutch East Indies</a> lay with the Royal Dutch East Indies Army and the Royal Netherlands Navy. They did not directly form the later Indonesian forces and were mainly the enemy during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indonesian-national-revolution">Indonesian National Revolution</a> of 1945 to 1949, but the Army had given military training to some future officers. It recruited Ambonese, Kai Islanders, Timorese and Minahasan people, and in 1940 it opened to large intakes of Javanese, among them the future officers <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/suharto">Suharto</a> and Abdul Haris Nasution.</p><p>The forces themselves were formed during the Revolution, when they fought a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guerrilla-warfare">guerrilla</a> war alongside informal militia. They began as the People's Security Agency on 22 August 1945, became the People's Security Forces on 5 October 1945 and then the Armed Forces of the Republic of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indonesia">Indonesia</a> in January 1946. The name Tentara Nasional Indonesia was first used on 3 June 1947. Later names included the War Forces of the Republic of Indonesia (1950 to 1962) and the Armed Forces of the Republic of Indonesia, ABRI (1962 to 1999). The name Tentara Nasional Indonesia was restored on 1 April 1999.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>The Army, Navy and Air Force have long been organised along territorial commands, a legacy of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guerrilla-warfare">guerrilla</a> war and of the need for internal security. Seven commands were established by 1958, and no central reserve formation existed until 1961. Only after the attempted coup of 1 October 1965 and General <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/suharto">Suharto</a>'s rise to the presidency did it become possible to integrate the services and develop a joint operations structure. A 1985 reorganisation separated the Ministry of Defense and Security from the ABRI headquarters, leaving the commander in chief in command of the forces and a member of the cabinet.</p><p>The military philosophy is civilian-military defence, called Total People's Defence. It foresees three stages: a short period in which an invader defeats the conventional army, a long period of territorial <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guerrilla-warfare">guerrilla</a> warfare, and a final expulsion, with the army as a rallying point from village level upwards. The Armed Forces Enters the Village programme, begun in 1983, is held three times a year to build civilian projects. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indonesia">Indonesia</a> has also sent forces to UN peacekeeping missions, including in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lebanon">Lebanon</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/democratic-republic-of-the-congo">Democratic Republic of Congo</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>The President of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indonesia">Indonesia</a> is Supreme Commander. Each service has a chief of staff, but only the commander of the armed forces holds command authority over all three. Special forces include the army's Kopassus and the navy's Kopaska. After the Surabaya bombings of 2018 President <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/joko-widodo">Joko Widodo</a> agreed to revive a joint special operations command, and in July 2019 he formed the Armed Forces Special Operations Command. In January 2021 a regulation created a reserve component, with voluntary membership.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Indonesian National Armed Forces” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Indonesian armed forces, today the <b>Tentara Nasional Indonesia</b> (TNI), were born in the revolution of 1945–1949, when regular units and irregular militias fought the Dutch with guerrilla tactics under commanders such as General Sudirman.</blockquote><a href="/empire/indonesia">Indonesia · Military</a></li></ul></section>
